## Is asthma a disability?

Asthma can count as a disability under the **Equality Act 2010** if it has a **substantial, long-term effect** on your daily life. When deciding, the law looks at how you would be **without your medicine**, so many people with asthma are covered.

## Reasonable adjustments at work

Your employer must consider **reasonable adjustments**, such as:

- moving you away from **dust, fumes or strong smells**
- a **smoke-free** workplace and changes to cleaning products
- **good ventilation** and temperature control
- time off for **appointments**
- somewhere to keep your inhaler

## Asthma caused by work

If your job causes your asthma or makes it worse, this is called **occupational asthma**. It is often linked to things like flour dust, wood dust, spray paints and some chemicals.

- tell your employer and your GP
- your employer has legal duties to **protect you** from harmful substances
- if you were an employee when you got it, you may be able to claim **Industrial Injuries Disablement Benefit**, as occupational asthma is a recognised work-related disease

## Benefits

PIP is based on how asthma affects you on **most days**. Severe asthma can affect activities such as:

- **moving around:** how far you can walk before breathlessness stops you, reliably and repeatedly
- **washing and dressing:** if steam, effort or lifting your arms makes you breathless
- **preparing food:** if you cannot stand at a cooker or you react to cooking fumes

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## Prescriptions

Asthma does not give you free prescriptions in England, but a **prescription prepayment certificate** can save money if you need several items. Prescriptions are **free in Wales**.
